A||||14||Robert||Why is there chocolate coming out of my steering wheel?||||5||
Z||000000||Robert||06-22-2000||09:03 PM||||Last week the temps got to 112 in my hometown of Pleasant Hill, CA.  Now my steering wheel is leaking “brown stuff” from where it is sewed together.  What the heck is this stuff and how do I stop it?   Is there a sealer that I could put on the sewing to seal it?  George at the “Fiero Shop” tells me that’s normal for the GT wheel in high temperature, and I should leave my sunroof open when I park.  Trouble is, the inside of my car gets dirty when I leave the window open. Anyone solved this problem before?<P>Thanks<BR>Robert<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gokart||06-22-2000||09:15 PM||||I know what you mean with dust/dirt when you leave the sunroof open. Try a sun-deflector. I got the kind that unfolds and I lay it across the windsheild from the inside of the car. I've noticed it is much cooler inside when I get in after work. Does a good job of protecting the dash as well.||||reg||2
Z||000002||batboy||06-22-2000||09:30 PM||||I love those leather GT steering wheels, but the older ones, and most are older now, do have a habit of oozing nasty gunk when it gets real hot. I just live with it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||06-22-2000||09:30 PM||||Robert, I had the same problem before I changed over to a marshmallow steering wheel, you may have noticed at the poker run that it was really hard for me to park in that spot without getting yellow paint on my car - that's because the wheel kept bending. Come to think of it, maybe you should get a wheel with white chocolate - then it wouldn't be so noticable. See? George doesn't know <I>everything!!!</I>||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||06-22-2000||09:31 PM||||and you'd better stop it soon before your car resembles a chocolate covered bananna!!! (hehehe)||||reg||1
